### What is the Balanced Parentheses problem?

Given a string containing only `()`, `[]`, and `{}`, determine whether every opener has a matching closer in the correct nesting order. The LIFO discipline of a stack makes it the natural tool: brackets must close in the reverse order they opened.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class BalancedParentheses {
    private static final Map<Character, Character> PAIRS =
            Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');

    public static boolean isValid(String s) {
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (PAIRS.containsValue(c)) {
                stack.push(c);
            } else if (PAIRS.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != PAIRS.get(c)) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isValid("({[]})")); // true
        System.out.println(isValid("({[)]"));  // false
        System.out.println(isValid("((("));    // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
false
```

### Why does this problem require a stack?

Brackets close in the reverse order they opened: last opened, first closed. This is exactly LIFO. A stack enforces that the most recently pushed element is the one you access next, which maps directly to the nesting rule.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class NestingOrder {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Open: { [ ( -> Stack: ( on top
        // Must close ( first, then [, then {
        Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push("{ opened"); stack.push("[ opened"); stack.push("( opened");

        System.out.println("Close order: " + stack.pop()); // ( first
        System.out.println("Close order: " + stack.pop()); // [ second
        System.out.println("Close order: " + stack.pop()); // { last
    }
}
```

```text
Close order: ( opened
Close order: [ opened
Close order: { opened
```

### How does the matching map work?

A `Map<Character, Character>` maps each closer to its expected opener. When you see `)`, look up `pairs.get(')')` which gives `(`. Compare that to `stack.pop()`. If they match, continue. If not, the string is invalid. This avoids a chain of if-else comparisons and is easy to extend.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Map;

public class MatchingMap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<Character, Character> pairs = Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');

        char closer = ')';
        char expected = pairs.get(closer);
        System.out.println("Closer: " + closer + " -> expected opener: " + expected);

        closer = '}';
        expected = pairs.get(closer);
        System.out.println("Closer: " + closer + " -> expected opener: " + expected);
    }
}
```

```text
Closer: ) -> expected opener: (
Closer: } -> expected opener: {
```

### What are the two failure conditions?

First: a closer arrives when the stack is empty, meaning there is no opener to match. Second: after the scan, openers remain on the stack, meaning they were never closed. Both checks are essential for correctness.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TwoFailures {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("')(' -> fails at index 0: stack empty on closer");
        System.out.println("'(((' -> scan finishes with 3 openers still on stack");
        System.out.println("'(]' -> fails at index 1: popped '(' does not match ']'");
        System.out.println("'()' -> both checks pass: valid");
    }
}
```

```text
')(' -> fails at index 0: stack empty on closer
'(((' -> scan finishes with 3 openers still on stack
'(]' -> fails at index 1: popped '(' does not match ']'
'()' -> both checks pass: valid
```

### What are the edge cases?

Empty string is valid. Odd-length string is always invalid (quick O(1) check). Single bracket is invalid. Deeply nested and sequential combinations must both pass.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ParenEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + BalancedParentheses.isValid(""));          // true
        System.out.println("Odd: " + BalancedParentheses.isValid("(()")); // false
        System.out.println("Deep: " + BalancedParentheses.isValid("(((())))")); // true
        System.out.println("Sequential: " + BalancedParentheses.isValid("()[]{}")); // true
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: true
Odd: false
Deep: true
Sequential: true
```

### What are common mistakes?

Forgetting the final `isEmpty()` check wrongly accepts all-opener strings. Not checking for an empty stack before popping throws `NoSuchElementException`. Not type-checking the match wrongly accepts crossed brackets like `"({)}"`.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ParenMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Bug 1: return true instead of stack.isEmpty()");
        System.out.println("  '(((' passes incorrectly");
        System.out.println("Bug 2: pop without empty check");
        System.out.println("  ')(' throws NoSuchElementException");
        System.out.println("Bug 3: pop without type check");
        System.out.println("  '({)}' passes incorrectly");
    }
}
```

```text
Bug 1: return true instead of stack.isEmpty()
  '(((' passes incorrectly
Bug 2: pop without empty check
  ')(' throws NoSuchElementException
Bug 3: pop without type check
  '({)}' passes incorrectly
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At the National Bank of India, structured transaction messages use nested bracket segments. A validator checks the brackets before the real parser runs. Malformed messages with crossed or dangling brackets are rejected to a dead-letter path, protecting the parser from errors and giving the operations team a clean audit trail.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TransactionMessageValidator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String good = "{header: [fields: (amount, currency)]}";
        String bad = "{header: [fields: (amount, currency]}";

        System.out.println("Good message: " + BalancedParentheses.isValid(
                good.replaceAll("[^()\\[\\]{}]", "")));
        System.out.println("Bad message: " + BalancedParentheses.isValid(
                bad.replaceAll("[^()\\[\\]{}]", "")));
    }
}
```

```text
Good message: true
Bad message: false
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

This technique is the foundation of real parsing. The identical approach validates HTML/XML tags, nested expressions, and balanced quotes. The map-based matching is trivially extensible to new bracket types. In production, early rejection of malformed input at the bracket level protects downstream parsers from wasting resources on corrupt data.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SeniorParenNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Extends to: HTML/XML tag matching, quote balancing");
        System.out.println("Map-based matching: add new bracket types with one entry");
        System.out.println("Production pattern: validate brackets before full parse");
        System.out.println("Prefer ArrayDeque over legacy Stack class");
    }
}
```

```text
Extends to: HTML/XML tag matching, quote balancing
Map-based matching: add new bracket types with one entry
Production pattern: validate brackets before full parse
Prefer ArrayDeque over legacy Stack class
```
